# Chroma library, modern Earth: LIFE EVENTS of work, money, body and the wider world (across stages). Plan: earth-plan.md "More life events".
# Syntax: header of build.py. Each event is even on its own: alpha even, stakes 1.0, the five one-color options plus its set; rates from Part D.
# Audit 2026-10-05 (drafts/audit/BRIEF.md): options checked for realism and age; child versions (variant_of) follow the events they split. Changes: earth-life-world-changes.md.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- life event: a breakthrough in your work (pairs D3a)

== a breakthrough in your work
tier: life event
at: employer
stages: juvenile+
age: 14-90
per_year: 0 .03 .03 .02 .02 .01
drivers: era+.3 ties+.3 money+.3 health+.2 stress-.3 harsh-.3 fortune+.15
gap: 3-8
times: repeats: about 3 in 100 children, teenagers and young adults, 2 in 100 adults and people in midlife, and 1 in 100 elders a year make a breakthrough of their own: a method that works, a finding, a talent discovered (D24 [est]); field-changing discoveries are very rare; opens at 14 (audit 2026-10-05): a child's first successes come through the child files (a gold star, an itch to build something, being picked for something special), and these acts (a mentor's verdict, doctored results, profit, putting a find on record) start with teenage projects
likelier: schooling, good tools, friends or a mentor who know the field, time and money to tinker, a curious bent
rarer: stress, low money, a harsh life, poor health, no time to think
domain: career.5
tone: joy
life: work, meaning
horizon: months
roles: mentor, colleague, boss, rival, friend
earth: after months of trying, the thing you have been working on finally works
scene: For months it would not come right. This afternoon, on the hundredth try, it works, and {N} sits very still, afraid to breathe on it.
scene W: {N} checks it the proper way, step by step, and it still holds. {mentor} should see this first, before anyone else does.
scene U: It works, and {N} can see why it works, which is better still. A dozen new questions open up behind it like doors.
scene B: Nobody else has this yet. {N} turns over what it could be worth, and to whom.
scene R: {N} whoops out loud, alone in the room, and wants to ring everyone at once.
scene G: It came the way good things grow, slowly and then all at once, like a seed that waited all winter in the ground.
- W | take it to your mentor first, and let them judge it before anyone else hears | v: tradition, conformity | chance: 60
- U | quietly leave out the one result that does not fit, so the find looks clean | v: achievement | mark: hid a wrong | closed: approval: doctored results; backfire: someone repeats the work, and the whole find falls apart | chance: 45
- B | keep it secret until you have worked out how to profit from it | v: power, achievement | chance: 55
- R | tell everyone you know tonight, without checking a thing | v: stimulation | chance: 58
- G | take it as a gift, and fold it quietly into the everyday work | v: tradition (humility), security | mark: turned down a chance | chance: 66
- WU | check it three times, write it up, and send it to the judges of such work | door | v: achievement, conformity | chance: 58
- UB | make sure it is on record under your own name before anyone can copy it | v: power, achievement | chance: 80
- WR | share the credit loudly with everyone who helped, and throw them a party | v: benevolence, hedonism | mark: made a friend | chance: 78
- RG | drop everything else and follow it wherever your gut leads | door | v: self-direction, stimulation | chance: 64
- BG | say nothing, and let it grow quietly until you are far ahead of the rest | v: power, achievement | chance: 55
works: What {N} found turns out to be real, and other people start to use it.
works: {N} is asked to show how it works, and the room goes quiet to listen.
fails: A week later it stops working, and {N} cannot make it happen again.
fails: Someone else gets there first, and the credit goes elsewhere.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- life event: an operation gives something back (cycle s3)
# Audit 2026-10-05: split at 12. This version is for 12 and over; "an operation that makes things better" (variant_of) is the child's.

== an operation gives something back
tier: life event
at: hospital
stages: all
age: 12-100
per_year: .003 .003 .003 .005 .015 .06
drivers: money+.4 era+.4 health+.2 harsh-.3 unrest-.3
gap: 2-5
times: repeats: about 3 in 1,000 children, teenagers and young adults, 5 in 1,000 adults, 1.5 in 100 people in midlife and 6 in 100 elders a year have an operation that gives back sight, hearing or a joint (D34 [est]), leaning on more than 3 million cataract operations and about 1 million hip and knee replacements a year in the US, mostly after sixty; under 12 the event comes as "an operation that makes things better", which carries the child rate (audit 2026-10-05)
likelier: money or good insurance, a hospital within reach, modern medicine, health good enough for surgery
rarer: low money, a harsh place, unrest that closes hospitals, health too poor to risk an operation
tone: joy
life: body, health
horizon: months
roles: partner, child, parent, friend, a surgeon
earth: a new hip, a cataract lifted, hearing restored: after the operation the body can do what it had stopped doing
scene: The operation is over, the dressings are off, and something that had been slipping away for years has been given back. {N} keeps testing it, as if it might vanish again.
scene W: The nurse hands over a printed sheet of exercises and check-ups: three times a day, for six weeks. {N} reads every line and means to keep to it.
scene U: {N} wants to know exactly what the surgeon did, and how a few millimetres of something new can change so much.
scene B: {N} has been given years back, years that were being taken away, and does not mean to waste a single one of them waiting on anyone.
scene R: {N} cannot stop grinning. The world feels wide open again, and {N} wants to be out in it today.
scene G: The body is knitting itself around the new part, the way bark closes over a cut branch. {N} lies still and lets it.
- W | follow the recovery plan to the letter, every exercise, every day | v: conformity, security | chance: 85
- U | read up on how the operation works, and ask the surgeon every question you have | v: self-direction | chance: 80
- B | take charge of your own recovery, and be back on your feet before anyone expects | v: achievement, self-direction | chance: 60
- R | go straight back to the thing you had given up, long before the doctor says | body: heavy | v: stimulation, hedonism | closed: approval: going against the surgeon's advice; backfire: a setback, and weeks lost | chance: 70
- G | take it slowly, and let the body heal at its own pace | v: security | chance: 79
- W>R | keep every rule of recovery, so you can run, dance or play again by summer | v: hedonism, conformity | chance: 66
- U>G | learn how the body heals, and work with it rather than against it | v: self-direction, universalism (nature) | chance: 75
- B>W | promise yourself a reward for every week the exercises get done | v: achievement, conformity | chance: 88
- R>U | test what the new body can do, a little further each day, to find its limits | body: light | v: stimulation, self-direction | chance: 88
- G>B | let the family carry everything for months, long after you need them to | v: hedonism | act: let the family carry everything for months, long after they need them to | chance: 48
works: Within weeks {N} is doing things that had slipped away years ago.
works: {N} wakes one morning and realises the old trouble has not crossed {Ns} mind for days.
fails: Recovery is slow and painful, and the gain is smaller than {N} was promised.
fails: An infection sets in, and {N} is back in hospital for a week.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- life event: an operation that makes things better (child version of "an operation gives something back"; cycle s3, same codes in the same order)

== an operation that makes things better
tier: life event
at: hospital
variant_of: an operation gives something back
stages: child, juvenile
age: 4-12
per_year: .003 .003 0 0 0 0
drivers: money+.4 era+.4 health+.2 harsh-.3 unrest-.3
gap: 2-5
times: the child slot of "an operation gives something back" (D34 [est]): about 3 in 1,000 children a year have an operation that gives back hearing, sight or an easy step, such as grommets, a squint put right or a foot set straight; the same event for once and rates, told at a child's height; the juvenile slot (3 in 1,000) is the original's, so an early juvenile of 10 to 12 meets this version until 12
likelier: money or good insurance, a hospital within reach, modern medicine
rarer: low money, a harsh place, unrest that closes hospitals
tone: joy
life: body, health
horizon: months
roles: parent, sibling, friend, a surgeon
earth: an operation in hospital, and afterwards you can hear, see or walk better than before
scene: Two days after the operation, {N} can hear the rain on the window for the first time in a year without straining, and keeps asking everyone to whisper, just to check.
scene W: The nurse gives {N} a sticker chart: drops twice a day, no swimming for a month. {N} takes it home and pins it up beside the bed.
scene U: {N} wants to see the pictures from inside: the tiny tube, the eardrum, how something so small can make the world so loud.
scene B: There were presents in hospital, there is ice cream at home, and everybody is being very nice. {N} could get used to this.
scene R: {N} can hear everything now, the birds, the television, a friend shouting from the end of the street, and wants to be out in all of it at once.
scene G: {N} lies in the garden with eyes shut, listening to the bees and the leaves, sounds that were there all along.
- W | keep every rule the nurse gave you, even the boring ones | v: conformity, security | chance: 82
- U | ask the doctor to show you the pictures, and explain what they did | v: self-direction | act: ask the doctor to show them the pictures, and explain what was done | chance: 80
- B | enjoy being fussed over, and ask for ice cream and the remote | v: hedonism, power | chance: 90
- R | race back out to play long before you are allowed | act: race back out to play long before they are allowed | body: heavy | v: stimulation, hedonism | closed: approval: going against the doctor's advice; backfire: a setback, and weeks lost | chance: 78
- G | rest, and let it mend in its own good time | v: security | chance: 90
- W>R | keep to the doctor's rules, so you can be out playing again by summer | v: hedonism, conformity | chance: 84
- U>G | ask how the body heals itself, and watch it get better day by day | v: self-direction, universalism (nature) | chance: 80
- B>W | bargain for a treat for every day you stick to the rules without a fuss | v: conformity, hedonism | chance: 90
- R>U | try out what you can do now, a little more each day, just to see | body: light | v: stimulation, self-direction | chance: 88
- G>B | let the family wait on you for weeks, long after you need it | v: hedonism | act: let the family wait on them for weeks, long after they need it | chance: 68
works: Within a few weeks {N} has forgotten there was ever anything wrong, and so has everyone else.
works: The teacher says {N} is a different child in class: listening, laughing, joining in.
fails: Getting better is slow and sore, and {N} misses the end of term.
fails: An infection sets in, and {N} is back in hospital for a few days.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- life event: earning a qualification (cycle s4)
# Audit 2026-10-05: split at 12. This version is for 12 and over; "the badge you worked for" (variant_of) is the child's.

== earning a qualification
tier: life event
stages: all
age: 12-85
per_year: .2 .2 .1 .03 .01 .003
drivers: money+.3 prosper+.3 community+.3 health+.2 stress-.3 unrest-.4 fortune+.1
gap: 1-3
times: repeats: about 5 in 100 children (a grade, a school stage), 20 in 100 teenagers (most at seventeen or eighteen, finishing school), 10 in 100 young adults, 3 in 100 adults, 1 in 100 people in midlife and 3 in 1,000 elders a year earn a qualification: a diploma, a degree, a licence, a trade certificate (D21 [est]), leaning on about 9 in 10 US young adults finishing secondary school and 4 in 10 holding a bachelor's degree by their late twenties; under 12 the event comes as "the badge you worked for", which carries the child rate (audit 2026-10-05)
likelier: money for fees, a growing economy, a community that values learning, good health, calm at home
rarer: low money, stress, poor health, caring duties, unrest that closes schools
rite: young_adult adult
domain: career.5
tone: joy
life: school, work
horizon: years
roles: parent, mentor, friend, grandparent, rival
earth: the results are in: the diploma, the licence or the certificate is yours, after years of work
scene: The envelope is thin, and {Ns} hands are not quite steady opening it. The certificate is inside, with {Ns} full name printed across the middle.
scene W: The names are read out in order. At {Ns} name, {N} walks up exactly as rehearsed, to shake the hand and take the paper.
scene U: The paper proves what {N} knows. {N} is already thinking about everything {N} does not know yet.
scene B: With this in hand, {N} can ask for more: a better place, better pay, a door that stayed shut last year.
scene R: {N} reads the result, drops the envelope on the floor, and runs to find {friend}.
scene G: {grandparent} never had the chance to sit for anything like this. {N} thinks of that, holding the paper.
- W | report the others you saw cheating, now that your own result is safe | v: conformity, universalism | mark: made an enemy | closed: approval: informing on classmates; backfire: the whole year shuts them out | chance: 38
- U | go straight on to the next level of study, years more of it | binds | v: achievement, self-direction | chance: 72
- B | use it at once to bargain for more: more money, more say, a better place | v: power, achievement | chance: 72
- R | throw the biggest party anyone can remember | v: hedonism, stimulation | chance: 78
- G | put the paper away in a drawer; the work matters, not the certificate | v: tradition (humility) | chance: 59
- W>G | hold a proper family celebration, with the grandparents in the place of honour | v: tradition, benevolence | chance: 80
- U>W | tutor the ones coming up behind, for free, with everything you learned | v: benevolence | chance: 71
- B>U | pull every string you can to get into the best course there is | door | v: achievement, power | chance: 42
- R>B | ride the high, and apply that same week for the next rung up | v: achievement, stimulation | chance: 55
- G>R | spend the whole summer outdoors before deciding anything at all | door | v: self-direction, hedonism | chance: 72
works: The paper opens a door that was shut before.
works: {N} hangs it on the wall, and for years catches sight of it with a quiet glow.
fails: Everyone else seems to have the same paper, and it counts for less than {N} hoped.
fails: The day itself goes sour, and {N} wonders what all the work was for.

