- G>B | claim the stranger as one of the family, a grown-up on your side | binds | v: security, tradition | mark: made a friend | chance: 48
works: The stranger comes to {Ns} birthday that year, and stays a friend of the family for years.
works: The fright fades, and what stays is a story {N} tells with a strange, bright pride.
fails: {N} dreams about the water for months, and will not go near the lake.
fails: The stranger slips away in the crowd, and {N} never learns their name.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- life event: a cause you fought for wins (the four-color set)

== a cause you fought for wins
tier: life event
stages: juvenile+
age: 12-95
per_year: 0 .01 .015 .01 .015 .01
drivers: community+.4 ties+.3 era+.2 prosper+.2 money+.2 health+.2 unrest-.4 fortune+.1
gap: 3-8
times: repeats: about 5 in 1,000 children, 2 in 100 teenagers, adults and elders, and 3 in 100 young adults and people in midlife a year see a cause they fought for win: a vote, a ruling, a park saved, a law changed (D39 [est]); opens at 12 (audit 2026-10-05): under 12 the cause is the grown-ups' or the school's, and the child files carry a child's stands (standing up for someone smaller, being picked for something special), so the child's 5 in 1,000 is left out. Point 12 (Emren 2026-10-05): the yearly figures above are lowered to 0, .01, .015, .01, .015, .01 (child to elder): only for people with a cause or a community role (the engine is asked for that gate).
likelier: a strong community, wide ties, an open era with free politics, a growing economy, money and health enough to keep going
rarer: repression and unrest, low money, poor health, isolation
domain: community.5 faith.5
tone: joy
life: community, public life
horizon: months
roles: friend, elder, rival, colleague, neighbours
earth: the vote comes in, and the thing you campaigned for has won
scene: The council votes eleven to nine, and the room erupts. Two years of leaflets, meetings and cold mornings on the town hall steps, and the cause {N} fought for has won.
scene W: The decision will be written into the rules, signed and stamped. {N} wants to see it kept, to the letter, by everyone.
scene U: {N} keeps going over how it was won: which argument turned which vote, and why it worked this time when it failed before.
scene B: The papers want a face for the win, and {N} is standing nearest the cameras.
scene R: {N} is hugging people {N} has never spoken to, and somebody at the back has started to sing.
scene G: {elder} began this fight long before {N} joined it. Now {elder} sits in the front row with eyes closed, like someone listening to rain after a long drought.
- W | see that the new rule is enforced to the letter on those who fought it | v: conformity, power | mark: made an enemy | closed: approval: using the win to punish neighbours; backfire: the neighbours never forgive it, and fight the next one harder | chance: 50
- U | study why it worked, and write it down for the next people who need to know | v: universalism, self-direction | mark: learned a skill | chance: 68
- B | take the credit in the papers, and use the name it makes you | identity | v: power, achievement | mark: made an enemy | act: take the credit in the papers, and use the name it makes them | chance: 55
- R | celebrate all night with everyone who marched | v: hedonism, benevolence | chance: 88
- G | go home, tend your own garden, and let others carry the movement on | v: tradition (moderation), security | mark: turned down a chance | chance: 45
- WUBG | keep the campaign going as a proper group, with a fund, a committee and a plan | binds | v: universalism, security | tags: commit:community | chance: 62
- WUBR | turn the win into a model: a toolkit, a website, the same fight in ten towns | v: universalism, achievement | tags: commit:community | chance: 35
- WURG | give the credit to everyone, and open the doors to anyone who wants to help | v: benevolence, universalism | tags: commit:community | chance: 78
- WBRG | strike again while the other side is reeling, and go straight after the next fight | v: power, achievement | tags: commit:faith | chance: 45
- UBRG | follow the energy into whatever cause catches fire next, and see where it goes | v: stimulation, self-direction | tags: commit:faith | chance: 50
works: A year later the change is real, and people who never heard of the campaign live by it.
works: The win draws new people in, and {N} finds a crowd of friends where there were strangers.
fails: Within months the decision is quietly watered down, and the fight starts again.
fails: The group splits over who did the most, and never meets again.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- life event: financial ruin (the four-color set)

== financial ruin
tier: life event
stages: young_adult adult mature elder
age: 18-95
per_year: 0 0 .0015 .003 .003 .0015
drivers: prosper-.6 money-.6 unrest+.4 health-.3 stress+.3 family-.2 trouble+.2
gap: 5-15
times: repeats, rarely: about 1.5 in 1,000 young adults, 3 in 1,000 adults and people in midlife, and 1.5 in 1,000 elders a year go bankrupt or sink under debts they cannot pay (D59 [data+est]: 533,949 US consumer bankruptcies in 2025, about 0.2 in 100 adults, peaking in middle age); the wider debt crises of 3 to 5 in 100 households a year come as a money squeeze, or as money running out at home in the teenage years
likelier: medical bills, a lost job, a divorce, gambling or addiction, a recession, unrest
rarer: money in the bank, health insurance, family who can help, a growing economy
tone: trouble
life: money, home, work
horizon: years
roles: partner, parent, child, friend, colleague
earth: the business fails or the debts pass the point of no return, and the court letter says bankrupt
scene: The letter from the court runs to two pages. Everything {N} owns is listed on the second page, and none of it is enough.
scene W: {N} owes money to people who trusted {N}: suppliers, a bank, {friend}. Every debt is a broken promise, and {N} feels each one.
scene U: {N} spreads every statement across the kitchen table and traces it back, month by month, to the first wrong turn.
scene B: There are rules in bankruptcy, and there are gaps in the rules. {N} has already started to see them.
scene R: {N} wants to smash something, shout at someone, and then stake the last of it on one throw.
scene G: {grandparent} lost everything once, in a bad year, and started again from one rented room. Families come through this.
- W | file for bankruptcy properly, declare everything, and start again from nothing | v: conformity, security | mark: owned up | chance: 70
- U | cook the books to hide how deep it goes, and buy a little time | v: security, power | mark: hid a wrong | closed: law: false accounts; backfire: the fraud is found, and the debt becomes a crime | chance: 35
- B | move what is left into a relative's name before the creditors come | v: power, security | mark: hid a wrong | closed: law: hiding assets from creditors; backfire: the court undoes the transfer, and adds a fine | chance: 58
- R | borrow from anyone who will lend, and stake it all on one last throw | binds | v: stimulation, power | mark: took a wild risk | chance: 12
- G | give up the home, move in with family, and live on very little | v: security, tradition | mark: moved away | chance: 72
- WUBG | agree a ten-year plan with every creditor, and pay each one back in full | binds | v: security, conformity | mark: kept your word | chance: 45
- WUBR | retrain in a new trade, and build a new business from the wreck of the old | door | v: achievement, self-direction | chance: 50
- WURG | pay the friends and small traders you owe first, and keep nothing back | identity | v: benevolence, universalism | chance: 70
- WBRG | fight every creditor in court, and keep your home at any cost | v: power (resources) | mark: made an enemy | chance: 30
- UBRG | walk away from the wreck, leave town, and start over where nobody knows you | v: self-direction | mark: left home | act: walk away from the wreck, leave town, and start over where nobody knows them | chance: 50
works: It takes years, but {N} stands up again, poorer and steadier.
works: Old friends turn up with small loans and large kindness, and {N} does not forget it.
fails: The debts follow {N} for a decade, into every new start.
fails: The shame of it closes {N} off, and some friendships do not survive.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- life event: losing the home (the four-color set)

== losing the home
tier: life event
stages: young_adult adult mature elder
age: 18-95
per_year: 0 0 .006 .0045 .0021 .0009
drivers: money-.6 stress+.3 era+.3 community-.3 family-.2 trouble+.2
gap: 3-8
times: repeats: per person about 2 in 100 young adults, 1.5 in 100 adults, 7 in 1,000 people in midlife and 3 in 1,000 elders a year lose their home to eviction or repossession (D60 [data+est]: eviction cases were filed against 6.1 in 100 US renter households and 2.3 in 100 were evicted in 2016, Eviction Lab); children and teenagers lose a home through their family, and meet it as the family's move to a new town or as money running out at home. Point 12 (Emren 2026-10-05): the yearly figures above are lowered to 0, 0, .006, .0045, .0021, .0009 (child to elder): eviction or repossession reaches about 1 in 5 people over a life (estimate from Eviction Lab filing rates of 2 to 3 in 100 renter homes a year); the old rate gave 6 in 10.
likelier: renting, low money, a lost job, a divorce, high rents, stress
rarer: owning outright, savings, family who can help, a close community, strong tenant law
moves: yes
tone: trouble
life: home, money
horizon: months
roles: partner, child, parent, friend, neighbours, a landlord
earth: the notice is on the door: thirty days to leave, and nowhere lined up to go
scene: The notice is taped to the door: thirty days. {N} stands in the hall reading it while the kettle boils, in a home that will not be {Ns} next month.
scene W: There are rules for this: a court date, a right to be heard, a proper process. {N} means to follow every step and make each one count.
scene U: {N} reads the tenancy agreement line by line at midnight, looking for the clause that changes everything.
scene B: The landlord wants the place empty so the rent can double. {N} knows exactly what it is worth to be rid of a tenant who will not leave.
scene R: {N} rips the notice off the door, then stands shaking in the hallway, too angry to think.
scene G: The worn step, the tree at the window, the pencil marks on the door frame: {N} has put down roots here, and now they are being pulled up.
- W | go to legal aid, pay what you can, and keep every letter in order | v: security, conformity | chance: 58
- U | dig through the tenancy law until you find the clause that buys more time | v: security, self-direction | mark: learned a skill | chance: 45
- B | strike a deal: leave early and quietly, in return for cash in hand | v: power, security | mark: moved away | chance: 78
- R | refuse to go, and bar the door when the bailiffs come | body: heavy | v: self-direction | mark: defied an authority | closed: law: resisting a lawful eviction; backfire: removed by force, with a fine and a record | chance: 25
- G | take any room at all in the same neighbourhood, to stay near your roots | v: tradition, security | mark: stayed home | chance: 62
- WUBG | move somewhere cheaper, save for years, and buy a small place nobody can take | binds | v: security, achievement | chance: 60
- WUBR | go where the work is, and rebuild from a single rented room in the city | v: achievement, self-direction | mark: moved away | chance: 65
- WURG | join the neighbours facing the same, and form a tenants' union together | v: universalism, benevolence | mark: made a friend | chance: 60
- WBRG | fight the landlord in court and on the doorstep until they back down | v: power (resources) | mark: made an enemy | chance: 40
- UBRG | sell almost everything, live out of a van, and see where the road goes | door | v: self-direction, stimulation | mark: left home | tech: car | chance: 68
works: {N} finds a new place before the month is out, smaller and somehow lighter.
works: The neighbours rally round, and {N} learns who will turn up with a van at seven in the morning.
fails: {N} spends months on friends' sofas, moving every few weeks.
fails: The eviction follows {N} onto every rental application for years.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- life event: war comes (the four-color set)

== war comes
tier: life event
stages: young_adult adult mature elder
age: 18-95
per_year: 0 0 .001 .001 .001 .001
drivers: unrest+2.5 harsh+.4 prosper-.4 era+.2 community-.2
times: rare in modern Earth: under 1 in 1,000 a year in a stable rich state, far more where the state is unstable (D79 [data+est]: 61 state-based conflicts were active in 36 countries in 2024, the most since 1946, so about one country in five had a war going on); opens at 18, because taking up arms is an adult's act here; a war in another country comes as news, not as this
likelier: unrest, a weak or failing state, scarcity, a hostile border, a shrinking economy
rarer: a strong and stable state, a growing economy, peace treaties, a close community
rite: young_adult adult
domain: community.5
tone: trouble
life: community, loss, home
horizon: years
roles: partner, parent, child, sibling, friend, neighbours, an officer
earth: the war is no longer on the news: the sirens sound in your own town, and the roads out are full
scene: The sirens go at four in the morning, and this time they are real. By noon the roads out of {place} are jammed, the shop shelves are bare, and the radio says the call-up starts on Monday.
scene W: The notice says everyone of fighting age must register by Monday. {N} reads it twice. When a country asks, its people answer.
scene U: {N} sits by the radio with a map, marking roads, bridges and the lines the reports describe, trying to see where this goes.
scene B: Fuel, medicine and batteries are worth three times what they were yesterday. {N} notices which neighbours have stockpiles, and which have nothing.
scene R: {Ns} blood is up. Someone is coming for {Ns} town, {Ns} people, {Ns} street, and {N} will not sit still for it.
scene G: {N} looks at the house, the garden, the old trees along the street. People have lived here through other wars, and stayed.
- W | report the neighbour you suspect of helping the other side | v: security, conformity | mark: made an enemy | closed: approval: informing on a neighbour on a suspicion; backfire: the neighbour is taken away, and the street never trusts them again | chance: 35
- U | keep your head down, hide the food, and plan every road out | v: security | chance: 65
- B | sell fuel and medicine under the counter, at whatever people will pay | v: power, achievement | mark: hid a wrong | closed: law: wartime profiteering; backfire: denounced, and the stock seized | chance: 62
- R | join the fighting at the front, as soon as they will take you | binds | body: heavy | v: security, stimulation | mark: took a wild risk | act: join the fighting at the front, as soon as they will take them | role: men | chance: 55
- G | bar the door, keep every scrap of food for your own, and turn strangers away | v: security | mark: refused someone in need | closed: approval: turning away people in need in wartime; backfire: shunned when their own turn comes | chance: 52
- WUBG | dig in for a long war: ration, store, and keep the household going for years | v: security, tradition | mark: stayed home | chance: 58
- WUBR | put your trade to work for the war: the factory, the radio room, the field hospital | v: security, achievement | chance: 68
- WURG | open your home to the wounded and the families fleeing, and share what you have | v: benevolence, universalism | mark: helped someone in need | chance: 68
- WBRG | take up arms with the neighbours, and hold the street yourselves | body: heavy | v: benevolence (in-group), power | role: men | chance: 42
- UBRG | slip across the border before the call-up, and start over wherever you land | v: self-direction, security | mark: left home | closed: law: evading the call-up; backfire: turned back at the border, and charged | law: -conscription | chance: 55
works: When the guns finally stop, {N} is still standing, and so are the people {N} loves most.
works: {N} comes through the war with a few friends for life, made in the worst of it.
fails: The war takes years from {N}, and the house with it.
fails: {N} comes out of it changed, and cannot settle for a long time.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- life event: a public scandal (cycle s2)

== a public scandal
tier: life event
