domain: community.3 career.3
tone: trouble
life: community, conflict
horizon: months
roles: friend, rival, colleague, boss, parent, partner
earth: something you said or did comes out, and by evening everyone you know has seen it
scene: By evening it is everywhere: the thing {N} said that was never meant to leave the room, passed from phone to phone. Conversations stop when {N} walks in.
scene W: {N} said it, and now everyone knows. The only decent way through is to own it, in front of the same people who saw.
scene U: {N} wants to know exactly how it got out: who saw it first, who passed it on, and why it spread so fast.
scene B: Somebody leaked this on purpose. {N} wants to know who gains from it, and how to turn it round.
scene R: {Ns} face burns. {N} wants to shout at every one of them that they have no idea what really happened.
scene G: It will pass, like weather. {N} has watched other people's storms blow over, and everyone forget.
- W | own up in public, and name everyone else who was in on it too | identity | v: conformity, universalism | mark: owned up | closed: approval: informing on the others; backfire: they turn on the one who named them, and the story doubles | chance: 42
- U | put out a careful account that tells the truth but leaves out the worst part | v: security | mark: hid a wrong | closed: approval: misleading people; backfire: the missing part comes out, and nobody believes a word after | chance: 55
- B | get your side out first, and turn the story so you look like the wronged party | v: power, security | mark: hid a wrong | chance: 60
- R | face them all down, head up, and refuse to hide | identity | v: self-direction | chance: 65
- G | stay out of sight for a while, and let it blow over | v: security | chance: 65
- W>B | get someone everyone respects to vouch for you in public | v: power, security | act: get someone everyone respects to vouch for them in public | chance: 52
- U>R | write your whole side of it, honestly and at length, where everyone can read it | identity | v: self-direction | chance: 45
- B>G | quietly call in favours until the group takes you back | v: security, power | act: quietly call in favours until the group takes them back | chance: 65
- R>W | go to each person you hurt, face to face, and say sorry | v: benevolence | mark: owned up | chance: 80
- G>U | talk it through with the oldest, wisest person you know, until you understand it | v: tradition, self-direction | chance: 72
works: Within a month people are talking about something else, and {N} can breathe again.
works: The friends who stay through it turn out to be the ones worth having.
fails: It follows {N} for years: every new person seems to have heard.
fails: {N} loses friends over it, and a place that mattered.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- life event: a heart attack or stroke (cycle s3)

== a heart attack or stroke
tier: life event
at: hospital
stages: young_adult adult mature elder
age: 20-100
per_year: 0 0 .0002 .001 .005 .015
drivers: health-.6 stress+.4 money-.3
gap: 2-5
times: repeats: about 2 in 10,000 young adults, 1 in 1,000 adults, 5 in 1,000 people in midlife and 1.5 in 100 elders a year have a heart attack or a stroke (D65 [est]), leaning on about 805,000 US heart attacks and 795,000 strokes a year, mostly after sixty
likelier: smoking, diabetes, high blood pressure, poor diet, stress, low money, age
rarer: exercise, good health care and the pills that keep blood pressure down, money
rite: elder
tone: trouble
life: body, health
horizon: months
roles: partner, child, friend, colleague, a doctor
earth: a crushing tightness in the chest, or a hand that will not work, and then an ambulance
scene: It starts as a tightness on the stairs, then {Ns} arm goes numb, and the next clear memory is the ceiling of the ambulance. A week later {N} is home, with a bag of pills and a leaflet.
scene W: The leaflet lists everything: the pills, the diet, the check-ups, the rehabilitation class on Thursdays. {N} reads it through twice, like a contract.
scene U: {N} wants the numbers: the blood pressure, the cholesterol, the odds for the next five years, and what moves them.
scene B: There are people who would step in fast if {N} looked weak. Nobody needs to know the whole of it.
scene R: {N} nearly went, and now every sound and taste is sharper. {N} wants to live every hour that is left, loudly.
scene G: The body has spoken plainly, in the only language it has. {N} lies still and listens.
- W | take every pill, keep every appointment, and keep to the diet to the letter | habit | v: security, conformity | chance: 68
- U | track every number, and test what brings each one down | habit | v: self-direction, security | chance: 65
- B | hide how bad it was, and go back early, so nobody thinks you are slipping | v: power, achievement | chance: 60
- R | go back to the old habits within the month; life is for living | habit | v: hedonism | closed: approval: against the doctors' orders; backfire: back in hospital within the year | chance: 50
- G | slow your whole life down: fewer hours, more walks, early nights | v: security | chance: 74
- W>R | join the rehabilitation class, do every drill, and enter a charity walk | v: achievement, stimulation | chance: 78
- U>G | learn to cook the way your grandparents ate, plain food made from scratch | v: tradition, security | chance: 65
- B>W | pay for the best private specialist, and get the whole family checked too | v: security | chance: 75
- R>U | climb the steepest hill around, to find out what the heart can take now | body: heavy | v: stimulation, self-direction | chance: 52
- G>B | throw away the pills and trust the old remedies, so nobody tells you what to do | v: self-direction, tradition | closed: approval: against medical advice; backfire: a second scare, and a worse one | act: throw away the pills and trust the old remedies, so nobody tells them what to do | chance: 35
works: Six months on, {N} walks further than before, and the numbers on the chart are falling.
works: {N} looks round the table at the people there and feels lucky in a way {N} never did before.
fails: Recovery is slow, and the old strength does not come back.
fails: A second scare puts {N} back in hospital within the year.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- life event: living with a chronic illness (cycle s4)
# Audit 2026-10-05: split at 12. This version is for 12 and over; "growing up with an illness" (variant_of) is the child's.

== living with a chronic illness
tier: life event
at: hospital
stages: all
age: 12-100
per_year: .006 .006 .009 .018 .03 .036
drivers: health-.5 money-.3 stress+.3 community-.2
gap: 8-15
times: repeats: about 1 in 100 children and teenagers, 1.5 in 100 young adults, 3 in 100 adults, 5 in 100 people in midlife and 6 in 100 elders a year learn they have an illness that will stay: diabetes, arthritis, asthma, a lung or bowel disease (D66 [est]), leaning on 6 in 10 US adults having at least one chronic disease and about 1.2 million new adult diabetes cases a year; an earlier warning about blood pressure and sugar comes as a warning from the doctor; under 12 the event comes as "growing up with an illness", which carries the child rate (audit 2026-10-05). Point 12 (Emren 2026-10-05): the yearly figures above are lowered to .006, .006, .009, .018, .03, .036 (child to elder): about 6 in 10 US adults live with a chronic disease and 4 in 10 with two or more (CDC); told at most every eight years.
likelier: age, inactivity and poor diet, low money, smoking, stress, a family history
rarer: good habits, money, modern treatments, a walkable town and an active community
tone: mixed
life: body, health
horizon: years
roles: parent, partner, friend, sibling, a doctor
earth: the tests come back: diabetes, arthritis or asthma, an illness with a name that will not go away
scene: The doctor says it gently: the illness has a name, it can be managed, and it will not go away. {N} walks out with a prescription, a leaflet and the rest of a life to live alongside it.
scene W: There will be a routine now: the medicine at eight, a check-up every three months, a list of things to avoid. {N} can keep a routine.
scene U: {N} goes home and reads everything: how it works, the trials, the forums, the numbers that matter.
scene B: {N} will not let this decide what {N} can and cannot do. If there is a way round it, {N} will find it.
scene R: {N} wants to tear the leaflet in half. {N} is not ill; {N} feels fine, mostly, most days.
scene G: Bodies change, the way trees lean with the wind. This is part of {Ns} body now, and {N} will have to learn its ways.
- W | learn the routine, and keep it every single day without fail | v: security, conformity | chance: 60
- U | read every study, and test on yourself what helps and what does not | v: self-direction | chance: 57
- B | fit it into your days on your own terms, and ask nobody for favours | v: self-direction | chance: 65
- R | ignore the diet and the pills, and carry on as before | v: hedonism, self-direction | chance: 28
- G | accept it as your lot, and make room for it without a fuss | v: tradition (accepting your portion) | chance: 62
- W>G | join the patients' group that meets every month, and become one of its regulars | v: conformity, benevolence | mark: made a friend | chance: 65
- U>W | learn the illness well, and help the newly diagnosed make sense of it | v: benevolence, universalism | chance: 65
- B>U | push the doctors hard until they put you on the newest treatment there is | v: power, achievement | act: push the doctors hard until they put them on the newest treatment there is | tech: modern medicine | chance: 75
- R>B | refuse to be slowed down, and train harder than anyone around you | body: heavy | v: achievement, power | act: refuse to be slowed down, and train harder than anyone around them | chance: 66
- G>R | go back to the outdoors, walk every day, and let the body find its own strength | habit | v: universalism (nature), hedonism | chance: 66
works: Within a year the routine is second nature, and most days {N} hardly thinks about it.
works: {N} learns the illness well enough to bend life around it, rather than the other way round.
fails: A bad spell lands {N} in hospital, and the illness takes a step forward.
fails: {N} grows tired of the pills and the rules, and the numbers slide.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- life event: growing up with an illness (child version of "living with a chronic illness"; cycle s4, same codes in the same order)

== growing up with an illness
tier: life event
at: hospital
variant_of: living with a chronic illness
stages: child, juvenile
age: 4-12
per_year: .006 .006 0 0 0 0
drivers: health-.5 money-.3 stress+.3 community-.2
gap: 8-15
times: the child slot of "living with a chronic illness" (D66 [est]): about 1 in 100 children a year learn they have an illness that will stay, most often asthma, type 1 diabetes, epilepsy or a serious allergy; the same event for once and rates, told at a child's height; the juvenile slot (1 in 100) is the original's, so an early juvenile of 10 to 12 meets this version until 12
likelier: a family history, a damp home or dirty air, low money, stress at home
rarer: money and good health care, a dry and healthy home, modern treatments
tone: mixed
life: body, health
horizon: years
roles: parent, grandparent, sibling, friend, a doctor
earth: the doctor explains that you have an illness that will need looking after every day
scene: The doctor crouches down to {Ns} height and draws it on a notepad: the illness has a name, there will be medicine every day, and it is nobody's fault. {parent} holds {Ns} hand very tightly.
scene W: There is a chart on the fridge now, with the times written in and a box to tick for each one. {N} likes the ticks, and makes sure none is ever missed.
scene U: {N} wants to know how the little machine at the clinic can tell what is going on inside, and asks the nurse to show exactly how it works.
scene B: Since the diagnosis {N} gets let off games, gets extra snacks and gets asked every day how {N} is feeling. Some of that, {N} decides, is not so bad.
