- U | ask the nurse how it all works, and learn to check it yourself | v: self-direction | chance: 64
- B | use it to get out of the lessons and jobs you do not like | v: hedonism, power | chance: 48
- R | skip the medicine when nobody is looking, and play as if nothing were wrong | v: hedonism, self-direction | chance: 22
- G | get used to it little by little, until it is just part of the day | v: tradition (accepting your portion) | chance: 78
- W>G | join the club for children with the same illness, and go every month | v: conformity, benevolence | mark: made a friend | chance: 68
- U>W | explain the illness to your class, so everyone knows what to do if needed | v: benevolence, universalism | chance: 64
- B>U | pester the grown-ups and the doctor until you get the newest gadget for it | v: power, hedonism | tech: modern medicine | chance: 68
- R>B | refuse to sit out, and race to beat everyone at sports day anyway | body: heavy | v: achievement, power | chance: 68
- G>R | play outdoors every spare hour, and let the body grow strong in its own way | habit | v: universalism (nature), hedonism | chance: 62
works: Within a few months the routine is just part of the day, and {N} hardly mentions it.
works: {N} knows the illness so well by now that {N} explains it to a new teacher without being asked.
fails: A bad spell means a night in hospital, and {N} misses the school trip everyone was talking about.
fails: {N} gets tired of the medicine and the rules, and every evening turns into a battle over them.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- life event: a long dark season (cycle s1)
# Audit 2026-10-05: split at 12. This version is for 12 and over; "a sadness that will not lift" (variant_of) is the child's.

== a long dark season
tier: life event
stages: all
age: 12-100
per_year: .018 .018 .015 .009 .006 .0045
drivers: stress+.6 ties-.4 money-.3 health-.3 community-.3 trouble+.2
gap: 2-5
times: repeats: about 2 in 100 children, 12 in 100 teenagers, 10 in 100 young adults, 6 in 100 adults, 4 in 100 people in midlife and 3 in 100 elders a year enter a major depressive episode (D68 [data+est]: 18.1 in 100 US 12 to 17 year-olds had one in the past year in 2023, NSDUH; episodes last months, so new episodes are set at about two thirds of the past-year figures); exhaustion from work comes as burnout; under 12 the event comes as "a sadness that will not lift", which carries the child rate (audit 2026-10-05). Point 12 (Emren 2026-10-05): the yearly figures above are lowered to .018, .018, .015, .009, .006, .0045 (child to elder): a life now holds about one on average: about 2 in 10 US adults recall a major depressive episode (Hasin 2018, NESARC-III), about twice that when followed up over years (Moffitt 2010), and about half of first episodes recur (Burcusa and Iacono 2007); the past-year figures counted the same people's episodes again and gave six a life.
likelier: stress, a loss, loneliness, low money, poor health, strife at home
rarer: close friends and family, exercise, a faith or a community, treatment
tone: trouble
life: mind, body
horizon: months
roles: friend, parent, partner, sibling, a doctor
earth: for weeks a heaviness has settled on everything: you sleep badly, eat little, and nothing you loved reaches you
scene: It has been weeks now. {N} wakes at four and cannot get back to sleep, cannot face breakfast, and cannot remember the last time anything was funny.
scene W: {N} keeps going in, keeps every appointment, keeps up appearances. Duty is the one thing that still gets {N} out of bed.
scene U: {N} notices the thoughts run round the same tracks every night, and tries to work out where the tracks begin.
scene B: {N} has stopped answering messages. Better to be alone than to let anyone see how weak {N} feels.
scene R: Some days {N} feels nothing at all; other days everything hurts at once, and {N} snaps at whoever is nearest.
scene G: It is like a winter that will not lift. {N} knows that winters end, but cannot feel it.
- W | keep every duty and appointment, and hold the days together by force of will | v: conformity, security | chance: 46
- U | keep a diary of every mood, and work out what sets it off | habit | v: self-direction | chance: 49
- B | cut everyone off, and rely on nobody but yourself | habit | v: security, self-direction | chance: 30
- R | stay up all night with loud music and screens, anything to stop thinking | habit | v: hedonism | chance: 24
- G | rest, eat what you can, and wait for the season to turn | v: security | chance: 48
- W>U | go to the doctor, and follow the treatment exactly as prescribed | v: security, conformity | chance: 65
- U>B | read up on the illness, and decide for yourself which treatment to take | v: self-direction, power | chance: 52
- B>R | drop the people and duties dragging you down, without explaining yourself | v: self-direction | mark: broke your word | act: drop the people and duties dragging them down, without explaining themselves | chance: 55
- R>G | tell a friend the whole raw truth of it, and let the friend stay close | v: benevolence | chance: 74
- G>W | lean on the family and its old routines, and let them carry the weight a while | v: tradition, security | chance: 57
works: Slowly, almost without noticing, {N} starts to laugh at things again.
works: One morning in spring {N} wakes and the weight has lifted a little, and then a little more.
fails: The dark season drags on for months, and {N} loses a term, a job or a friend to it.
fails: It lifts, then comes back in the autumn, heavier.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- life event: a sadness that will not lift (child version of "a long dark season"; cycle s1, same codes in the same order)

== a sadness that will not lift
tier: life event
variant_of: a long dark season
stages: child, juvenile
age: 8-12
per_year: .003 .006 0 0 0 0
drivers: stress+.6 ties-.4 money-.3 health-.3 community-.3 trouble+.2
gap: 2-5
times: the child slot of "a long dark season" (D68 [data+est]): about 2 in 100 children a year enter a depressive episode, mostly from eight or nine on, often showing as tiredness, stomach aches and a short temper more than as sadness; the same event for once and rates, told at a child's height; the juvenile slot (12 in 100) is the original's, so an early juvenile of 10 to 12 meets this version until 12
likelier: stress or strife at home, a loss, being left out or picked on at school, poor health, low money
rarer: close family and friends, play and exercise, a caring school, early help
tone: trouble
life: mind, body
horizon: months
roles: parent, sibling, friend, grandparent, a teacher, a doctor
earth: for weeks you have felt tired and low, nothing is fun any more, and you cannot say why
scene: For weeks now {N} has not wanted to play. The ball stays in the shed, the stomach aches come every school morning, and {N} cannot say what is wrong, only that everything has gone grey.
scene W: {N} still does the homework and still goes to school every day, because that is what is expected. It just takes everything {N} has.
scene U: {N} lies awake trying to work out why everything feels so heavy, when nothing bad has even happened.
scene B: {N} has started saying "fine" to everyone, in just the right voice. It is easier than anyone knowing.
scene R: Some days {N} cries over nothing; other days {N} snaps at {sibling} over nothing at all, and then feels worse.
scene G: It is like a long grey winter inside. {N} sits on the windowsill and watches the rain, waiting for something to change.
- W | keep going to school and doing the homework, even on the worst days | v: conformity, security | chance: 45
- U | draw or write how each day feels, to find out what makes it worse | habit | v: self-direction | chance: 50
- B | tell everyone you are fine, and keep to your room | habit | v: security, self-direction | chance: 26
- R | play games on a screen late into the night, anything not to think | habit | v: hedonism | chance: 22
- G | rest, curl up somewhere cosy, and wait for the grey to pass | v: security | chance: 46
- W>U | tell a parent or a teacher, and go with them to see the doctor | v: security, conformity | chance: 66
- U>B | look up why people feel like this, and work out your own ways to cope | v: self-direction, power | chance: 45
- B>R | quit the clubs and lessons that feel like too much, and refuse to explain | v: self-direction | mark: broke your word | chance: 50
- R>G | tell a friend how it really feels, and let them stay close | v: benevolence | chance: 72
- G>W | stay close to the family, and let the everyday routines carry you along | v: tradition, security | act: stay close to the family, and let the everyday routines carry them along | chance: 60
works: One Saturday {N} asks to go out to play, for the first time in weeks.
works: With help the grey lifts slowly, and by spring {N} is laughing with {friend} again.
fails: The grey drags on for months, and {N} falls behind at school and drifts away from friends.
fails: It lifts in the summer, then comes back with the dark evenings, heavier.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- life event: an addiction takes hold (cycle s3)

== an addiction takes hold
tier: life event
stages: juvenile young_adult adult mature elder
age: 14-100
per_year: 0 .006 .01 .005 .002 .001
drivers: stress+.5 money-.3 era+.3 unrest+.3 community-.3 ties-.3 health-.2 trouble+.2
gap: 2-5
times: repeats: about 3 in 100 teenagers, 5 in 100 young adults, 2.5 in 100 adults, 1 in 100 people in midlife and 5 in 1,000 elders a year find that a habit (drink, pills, a drug, betting) has taken hold of them (D69 [data+est]: a past-year substance use disorder in 8.3 in 100 US 12 to 17 year-olds and 26.9 in 100 at 18 to 25, NSDUH 2022 to 2024; the onset rates are estimated); the everyday pull of a craving comes as an inner moment. Point 12 (Emren 2026-10-05): the yearly figures above are lowered to 0, .006, .01, .005, .002, .001 (child to elder): about 3 in 10 US adults meet an alcohol use disorder at some point and 1 in 10 a drug use disorder (Grant 2015, 2016, NESARC-III), most of them mild; the past-year figures gave nearly every life one.
likelier: friends who use, stress, an old hurt, low money, a family history, easy supply, unrest
rarer: a close community, a faith, good health, friends who do not use, money and a full life
tone: trouble
life: body, health
horizon: years
roles: friend, partner, parent, sibling, colleague, a doctor
earth: one morning you count back and see it: the drink, the pills or the bets are no longer something you choose
scene: {N} counts back over the week and sees it plainly for the first time: every day, a little more, and the days without it are the worst of all. It is not a choice any more.
scene W: {N} has always kept the rules, and this breaks every one of them. The shame of that sits heavier than the habit itself.
scene U: {N} has a theory about why it is all under control. The theory has more holes in it every week.
scene B: {N} can handle it. {N} has handled everything else. Nobody needs to know, as long as {N} is careful.
scene R: It is the one bright thing in a flat week, and {N} is not ready to give it up.
scene G: It runs in the family: {parent} had the same hunger, and {N} always swore it would stop here.
- W | promise someone you trust that you will stop, and check in every week | binds | v: conformity, security | chance: 36
- U | invent a cover story for every missing hour and every missing coin | v: hedonism, security | mark: hid a wrong | closed: approval: lying to the people closest; backfire: the stories unravel all at once | self_control: - | chance: 12
- B | tell yourself you can manage it, and arrange your days around it | habit | v: hedonism, self-direction | chance: 20
- R | chase the high again tonight, and worry about it tomorrow | habit | v: hedonism, stimulation | chance: 10
- G | stop for the sake of the family, starting today | v: benevolence, tradition | chance: 35
- W>R | join a twelve-step group, and follow the steps to get your life back | habit | v: conformity, self-direction | chance: 40
- U>G | go on a doctor's programme, and cut down slowly over months | binds | v: security | chance: 50
- B>W | strike a bargain with yourself: strict limits, and weekends only | v: security, hedonism | chance: 38
- R>U | quit overnight, tell everyone, and find out what you are made of | v: self-direction, stimulation | mark: owned up | self_control: + | chance: 42
- G>B | close ranks with the family, and shut out anyone who asks questions | v: security | mark: hid a wrong | closed: approval: hiding it from those who could help; backfire: it grows in the dark, and help comes late | chance: 15
works: With help, {N} gets free of it, one day and then another.
works: {N} stops, slips, and stops again, and this time it holds.
fails: The habit tightens its grip, and {N} starts to lose the things that mattered.
fails: {N} stops for a month, then starts again, and stops telling anyone.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- added 2026-10-05 06:40 at the engine's pull-in (fix 5)
# Finding work again after a job loss (the engine gates it on a lost job). Pairs D4b. Every option takes the job.

== a new job at last
tier: life event
at: employer
stages: young_adult adult mature elder
age: 17-70
per_year: 0 0 4.4 4.4 3.6 1.4
drivers: prosper+.5 ties+.3 health+.2 unrest-.4 fortune+.1
excludes: career
domain: career1
times: between jobs, about 6 in 10 find work again within three months and 9 in 10 within a year, fewer in a recession, after a long gap and after sixty (the engine's own rate, earth_rules.FIXES, 2026-10-05 14:00; D13 counts any new job); the engine offers it only after a lost job, since a first job comes through the young-adult and juvenile events
likelier: a growing economy, contacts who hear of openings, skills in demand, good health, a town with work in it
rarer: a recession, unrest, a closing trade, poor health, age past sixty, a long gap already
tone: joy
life: work, money
horizon: years
roles: boss, colleague, partner, parent
earth: a phone call: the job is yours, starting Monday
scene: After months of applications and silence, the phone rings: {boss} wants {N} to start on Monday.
scene W: A proper contract, a start date and a place to be each morning. {N} irons a shirt the night before.
scene U: {N} reads the job description again, trying to see what the work will actually teach.
scene B: There is a second interview next week with a firm that pays more. {N} is in no hurry to sign anything.
scene R: The months of waiting are over. {N} wants to shout it out of the window and start tomorrow.
scene G: {N} thinks of {parent}, who did the same work for thirty years, and wonders if this job could be like that.
- W | accept the first decent offer, and turn up early every day | habit | v: conformity, security | tags: commit:career | chance: 78
- U | take the job with the most to learn, even for less pay | door | v: self-direction, achievement | tags: commit:career | chance: 70
- B | play the two offers against each other until one pays properly | v: power (resources), achievement | tags: commit:career | chance: 75
- R | take the job in a new city on the spot, and pack that night | door | v: stimulation | mark: moved away | tags: commit:career | chance: 73
- G | go back to the line of work you know, close to home, for good | binds | v: tradition, security | tags: commit:career | chance: 85
- BR | sell the months out of work as a bold year of travel, a little stretched | v: power, stimulation | mark: hid a wrong | closed: approval: stretching the truth on a CV; backfire: caught out at the reference check | tags: commit:career | chance: 72
- UB | get in through someone who owes a favour, and study the place before day one | v: power, achievement | tags: commit:career | chance: 82
- UG | retrain for steadier work close to home, and take the first post it offers | door | binds | v: security, self-direction | tags: commit:career | chance: 60
- WG | join a cooperative where everyone shares the work and the pay | binds | v: universalism, benevolence | tags: commit:career | chance: 66
- WR | take the hardest job going, the night shift on the ambulances | body: light | identity | v: benevolence, stimulation | tags: commit:career | chance: 62
works: The first week goes well, and by Friday {colleague} has shown {N} where the good coffee is.
works: The pay comes in at the end of the month, and the household breathes out.
fails: The job is not what was promised, and the first weeks drag.
fails: {N} starts on the back foot, and the team has already decided who the newcomer is.
